## How To Work Here

- Make new projects under `/home/{{USERNAME}}/projects` unless the user gives a different location.
- Create the project directory if it does not exist.
- For new projects, initialize a git repository and create a private GitHub repository with `gh` as early as practical.
- Prefer the toolchain already installed on this box before adding new tools.
- For GitHub work, use the GitHub CLI (`gh`) when possible.
- Before changing a repo, inspect its existing README, package files, task runner, tests, and local conventions.
- Run the smallest useful verification after changes. If you cannot verify something, tell the user what remains unverified.
- Do not expose secrets in chat, logs, commits, screenshots, or generated files.
- Ask the user before destructive actions, force pushes, deleting data, changing billing/account settings, or widening public access.

## New Web Projects

When the user asks for a new web-based project, aim to get a live private preview online quickly:

- Create the project under `/home/{{USERNAME}}/projects` unless the user gives a different location.
- Initialize git immediately, make an early first commit once the scaffold runs, and keep committing meaningful progress.
- Create a private GitHub repository with `gh`, add it as the remote, and push the branch as soon as practical.
- Create a project subdomain in the configured Cloudflare zone, route it through the existing tunnel, and add the matching cloudflared ingress rule for the local app port.
- Subdomain naming controls access: a plain name (e.g. `myapp.{{CF_ZONE_NAME}}`) is caught by the `*.{{CF_ZONE_NAME}}` Cloudflare Access wildcard and requires login; a `public-<name>.{{CF_ZONE_NAME}}` is matched by the bypass app and is openly reachable. Default to the protected name. Only use `public-` after explicit user confirmation, and tell them the URL will be world-readable.
- Share the live URL with the user as soon as there is a minimal working page, then continue iterating from their feedback.

## Existing Web Projects

When the user asks you to clone or work on an existing project:

- Clone it under `/home/{{USERNAME}}/projects` unless the user gives a different location.
- Inspect the README, package files, scripts, and framework conventions before choosing commands.
- If it runs a local web server, start it on a stable local port, create a project subdomain in the configured Cloudflare zone, route it through the existing tunnel, and add the matching cloudflared ingress rule.
- Share the live URL with the user once the project is running so they can review it in a browser.
- Commit changes on a branch, push that branch when useful, and open a pull request for review. Check with the user before pushing directly to shared or default branches.

## Testing And CI

- Add or update tests for meaningful behavior changes, especially user-facing web flows, data handling, authentication, and deployment logic.
- Run the relevant test and lint commands before reporting work as complete. If no commands exist, inspect the project and add sensible ones where practical.
- For new GitHub projects, set up lightweight CI with GitHub Actions to run tests and linting on pull requests and pushes.
- For existing projects, preserve the current test stack and CI conventions. Extend them when useful, but avoid replacing working project tooling without a clear reason.
- If a project has no test coverage yet, start with a small smoke test or component/unit test that proves the main path works.

## First Prompt

On the first prompt in a fresh session outside an existing project directory, briefly explain that you can help create a new web project with a live private URL or work on an existing repository. Ask the user which they want to do, unless they have already made that clear. If the session starts inside a project/repo, skip this opener and focus on that project.

